      View the captured data in the <code>./output/flow/&lt;capture_date_time&gt;.json</code> JSON file, which contains JSON arrays of the captured data.
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n  \"AgentIP\": \"10.0.1.76\",\n  \"Bytes\": 561,\n  \"DnsErrno\": 0,\n  \"Dscp\": 20,\n  \"DstAddr\": \"f904:ece9:ba63:6ac7:8018:1e5:7130:0\",\n  \"DstMac\": \"0A:58:0A:80:00:37\",\n  \"DstPort\": 9999,\n  \"Duplicate\": false,\n  \"Etype\": 2048,\n  \"Flags\": 16,\n  \"FlowDirection\": 0,\n  \"IfDirection\": 0,\n  \"Interface\": \"ens5\",\n  \"K8S_FlowLayer\": \"infra\",\n  \"Packets\": 1,\n  \"Proto\": 6,\n  \"SrcAddr\": \"3e06:6c10:6440:2:a80:37:b756:270f\",\n  \"SrcMac\": \"0A:58:0A:80:00:01\",\n  \"SrcPort\": 46934,\n  \"TimeFlowEndMs\": 1709741962111,\n  \"TimeFlowRttNs\": 121000,\n  \"TimeFlowStartMs\": 1709741962111,\n  \"TimeReceived\": 1709741964\n}"} lang="json" title="Example JSON file" />
      </li>
      <li>
      You can use SQLite to inspect the <code>./output/flow/&lt;capture_date_time&gt;.db</code> database file. For example:
